00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:07.730
I remember when I first learned about vectorization, I spent many hours on my computer taking

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:12.810
an unvectorized version of an algorithm running it, see how long I ran, and then running a

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.210
vectorized version of the code and seeing how much faster that ran.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.530
And I just spent hours playing with that.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:24.150
And it frankly blew my mind that the same algorithm vectorized would run so much faster.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.050
It felt almost like a magic trick to me.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.330
In this video, let's figure out how this magic trick really works.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:36.010
Let's take a deeper look at how a vectorized implementation may work on your computer behind

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.250
the scenes.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.370
Let's look at this for loop.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.110
A for loop like this runs without vectorization.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:52.590
So if J ranges from zero to say 15, this piece of code performs operations one after another.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:58.330
On the first time step, which I'm going to write as time zero or t zero, it first

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:02.130
operates on the values at index zero.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:08.450
At the next time step, it calculates values corresponding to index one, and so on until

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.510
the 15th step where it computes that.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:21.010
In other words, it calculates these computations, one step at a time, one step after another.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:28.610
In contrast, this function in NumPy is implemented in the computer hardware with vectorization.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:33.550
So the computer can get all values of the vectors w and x.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:39.950
And in a single step, it multiplies each pair of w and x with each other all at the same

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.450
time in parallel.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:47.870
Then after that, the computer takes these 16 numbers and uses specialized hardware to

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:54.010
add them all together very efficiently, rather than needing to carry out distinct additions

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:58.330
one after another to add up these 16 numbers.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:03.750
This means that code with vectorization can perform calculations in much less time than

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.510
code without vectorization.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:11.050
And this matters more when you're running learning algorithms on large data sets, or

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:16.350
trying to train large models, which is often the case with machine learning.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.810
So that's why being able to write vectorized implementations of learning algorithms has been

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:26.570
a key step to getting learning algorithms to run efficiently and therefore scale well

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:32.430
to the large data sets that many modern machine learning algorithms now have to operate on.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:38.010
Now let's take a look at a concrete example of how this helps with implementing multiple

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:43.770
linear regression, that is linear regression with multiple input features.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:52.850
Say you have a problem with 16 features and 16 parameters w1 through w16, in addition

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.530
to the parameter b.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:00.450
You calculated 16 derivative terms for these 16 weights.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:07.450
And in code, maybe you stored the values of w and d in two numpy arrays, with d storing

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.690
the values of the derivatives.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.890
For this example, I'm just going to ignore the parameter b.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:20.410
Now you want to compute an update for each of these 16 parameters.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:32.570
So wj is updated to wj minus the learning rate, say 0.1 times dj for j from 1 through

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:33.970
16.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:40.650
In code, without vectorization, you would be doing something like this.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:49.370
Update w1 to be w1 minus the learning rate 0.1 times d1, next update w2 similarly, and

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:57.770
so on through w16, updated as w16 minus 0.1 times d16.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:06.090
In codes without vectorization, you could use a for loop like this for j in range 0.16,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:14.330
that again goes from 0 to 15, said wj equals wj minus 0.1 times dj.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:20.850
In contrast, with vectorization, you can imagine the computer's parallel processing hardware

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:21.950
like this.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:31.330
It takes all 16 values in the vector w and subtracts in parallel 0.1 times all 16 values

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:39.050
in the vector d and assign all 16 calculations back to w, all at the same time and all in

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.370
one step.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:45.170
In code, you can implement this as follows.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:50.170
W is assigned to w minus 0.1 times d.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:56.290
Behind the scenes, the computer takes these numpy arrays, w and d, and uses parallel processing

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:00.950
hardware to carry out all 16 computations efficiently.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:05.530
So using a vectorized implementation, you should get a much more efficient implementation

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.730
of linear regression.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:13.130
Maybe the speed difference won't be huge if you have 16 features, but if you have

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:18.170
thousands of features and perhaps very large training sets, this type of vectorized implementation

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:21.970
will make a huge difference in the running time of your learning algorithm.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:26.690
It could be the difference between code finishing in one or two minutes versus taking many

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.610
many hours to do the same thing.

